## Overview

Continuous Integration and Continuous Delivery (CI/CD) pipelines run automated build and test workflows on every commit. When a workflow fails, developers wait longer for feedback, compute resources are consumed, and work is blocked until the cause is found.

This project asks one question:

> Before a CI workflow run starts, can we estimate how likely it is to fail, using only information available at that moment?

If failure risk can be estimated in advance, reviewers and pipeline owners can direct attention to the riskiest runs first.

## Why this project

Earlier studies have predicted CI build outcomes with machine learning, but their results are often hard to trust:

- Random train/test splits mix past and future builds.
- Some features are only known after a build starts (data leakage).
- Models are rarely compared with a simple baseline, such as each repository's own historical failure rate.
- Failures have many causes (code regressions, flaky tests, infrastructure and configuration problems), so a prediction is not a statement about code quality.

This project addresses these gaps by **fixing its evaluation rules before testing**:

- A go/no-go data audit
- A strict prediction-time definition
- Time-based and unseen-repository holdouts
- An acceptance criterion measured against the historical-rate baseline and the best possible ranking, with confidence intervals

## Application areas

| Area | What it covers |
| --- | --- |
| Software engineering analytics | Study CI workflow histories, commits and repository metadata from public projects |
| Supervised machine learning | Predict workflow failure with Logistic Regression, Random Forest and gradient boosting, against strong baselines |
| Temporal data engineering | Build leakage-safe historical features that never use information from after the prediction time |
| Evaluation and MLOps | Evaluate on future builds and unseen repositories, calibrate risk scores, and deliver an inspectable service |

## Objectives

1. **Audit the dataset.** Audit a public CI dataset (outcome labels, timestamps, identifiers, feature availability) and apply a written go/no-go gate before any modelling.
2. **Define the prediction contract.** Build a leakage-safe feature pipeline in which every run uses only information available at its prediction time, verified by automated tests.
3. **Compare models and baselines.** Compare a dummy baseline, Logistic Regression, Random Forest and HistGradientBoosting with a smoothed historical repository failure-rate baseline, using Average Precision and recall at a 10% alert budget.
4. **Evaluate generalization.** Evaluate on future builds and on repositories unseen in training, with calibration, feature-group ablation and error analysis.
5. **Deliver the application.** Deliver a FastAPI service and a two-page Streamlit dashboard for inspecting historical builds, together with a model card that states the limitations.

## Approach

### Prediction contract

Every prediction is made at a defined prediction time. Only information available at that time may be used. Repository history is reconstructed only from runs that had **already finished**. Automated tests verify that no feature uses later information.

### Models

| Model | Role |
| --- | --- |
| Dummy baseline | Floor for comparison |
| Smoothed historical repository failure rate | Strong, simple baseline |
| Logistic Regression | Interpretable linear model |
| Random Forest | Tree ensemble |
| HistGradientBoosting | Gradient boosting |

### Evaluation

- **Average Precision** for ranking quality on imbalanced data
- **Recall at a 10% alert budget**: the share of all real failures caught when only the top 10% riskiest runs are flagged
- **Temporal holdout**: train on earlier builds, test on later builds
- **Unseen-repository holdout**: test on repositories not seen in training
- **Calibration**: check that risk scores behave like probabilities
- **Feature-group ablation** and **error analysis**
- **Repository-clustered bootstrap confidence intervals**

### Success criterion (fixed in advance)

- Recall at the 10% alert budget of **at least 0.20**
- Comparison against the historical-rate baseline using repository-clustered bootstrap confidence intervals
- A missed target will be reported honestly as a finding.

## Scope

**In scope**

- Dataset audit, feature engineering, model development and evaluation
- Workflow-level failure prediction (success versus failure) from pre-build metadata
- Prediction service (FastAPI) and dashboard (Streamlit)
- Model card, Docker packaging and automated CI

**Out of scope**

- Live integration with GitHub
- Identifying the cause of a failure or labelling code as defective
- Automatic build cancellation
- Deep-learning models
- CatBoost / LightGBM
- Free-form what-if predictions

## Deliverables

- Go/no-go data audit with a recorded decision and reasoning
- Leakage-safe feature pipeline with automated tests
- Trained and tuned models with comparison against baselines
- Evaluation report: temporal and unseen-repository results, calibration, ablation, error analysis, feature importance
- FastAPI prediction service
- Two-page Streamlit dashboard for inspecting historical builds
- Model card stating the limitations
- Dockerized application with automated CI
- Final dissertation

